rtlwifi: quiesce the chip when the ISR runs while driver-disabled (issue #99)

_rtl_pci_interrupt() returns IRQ_HANDLED immediately when rtlpci->irq_enabled
is 0, without touching the chip. With a level-triggered INTA behind the
RTL8196E's cascaded interrupt controller that is fatal: disable_interrupt()
zeroes HIMR from process context, but the RTL8192EE keeps INTA asserted for a
HISR bit already set at that moment, the intc acks its own latch and unmasks,
the line is still high, and the CPU re-takes it before the interrupted
context executes one instruction. Measured on hardware with per-branch ISR
counters: 6,582,638 ISR entries during one AP start, 6,582,535 of them through
this early return, ~130 kHz, HISR=ROK, enable/disable calls balanced (the last
was a disable). Every softirq starves, the timer wheel stops, and the
softlockup detector panics ~20 s later -- the "hostapd AP start freezes the
board" bug.

Read and write-1-clear HISR/HISRE in that path (dispatching nothing), so the
line drops and the process context can finish and re-enable. Guarded by
driver_is_goingto_unload for teardown. The raw register offsets are 8192ee's;
a hal op would be the upstream shape. Also records a snapshot of the chip on
the first such entries for the write-up. Applies after
rtlwifi-zdebug-isr-counters (shares its counters).

diff -Naur a/drivers/net/wireless/realtek/rtlwifi/pci.c b/drivers/net/wireless/realtek/rtlwifi/pci.c
--- a/drivers/net/wireless/realtek/rtlwifi/pci.c	2026-09-01 16:31:37.781227528 -0300
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/realtek/rtlwifi/pci.c	2026-09-01 16:31:37.793227948 -0300
@@ -858,6 +858,44 @@
 	rtl_pci_dbg.isr_calls++;
 	if (rtlpci->irq_enabled == 0) {
 		rtl_pci_dbg.not_enabled++;
+		/*
+		 * Our own level-triggered INTA, with the driver side disabled:
+		 * disable_interrupt() zeroes HIMR/HIMRE and clears irq_enabled
+		 * from process context (hw reconfiguration paths do this around
+		 * their work). On the RTL8196E + RTL8192EE the chip keeps INTA
+		 * asserted for a HISR bit that was already set when HIMR was
+		 * zeroed, so returning here without touching it leaves the line
+		 * high: the cascade re-delivers it on the very next instruction,
+		 * forever -- measured as 6.5M entries through this branch at
+		 * ~130 kHz, every softirq starved, the process context that would
+		 * call enable_interrupt() never running again, softlockup panic
+		 * ~20 s later. Quiesce the source instead: read and write-1-clear
+		 * the status registers, dispatching nothing (the driver asked not
+		 * to be told; it rescans the rings on its next real interrupt once
+		 * it re-enables). Skipped once teardown has begun and the chip may
+		 * already be powered down.
+		 */
+		if (!rtlpci->driver_is_goingto_unload) {
+			/* 8192ee: HIMR 0xB0, HISR 0xB4, HIMRE 0xB8, HISRE 0xBC */
+			u32 hisr = rtl_read_dword(rtlpriv, 0x00B4);
+			u32 hisre = rtl_read_dword(rtlpriv, 0x00BC);
+
+			if (rtl_pci_dbg.not_enabled <= 2) {
+				rtl_pci_dbg.ne_hisr = hisr;
+				rtl_pci_dbg.ne_hisre = hisre;
+				rtl_pci_dbg.ne_himr = rtl_read_dword(rtlpriv, 0x00B0);
+				rtl_pci_dbg.ne_himre = rtl_read_dword(rtlpriv, 0x00B8);
+			}
+			/* the hal op clears the bits the driver normally handles */
+			rtlpriv->cfg->ops->interrupt_recognized(hw, &intvec);
+			/* ...and drop anything outside irq_mask too, so the line
+			 * cannot stay up on a bit the driver never dispatches */
+			if (hisr & ~intvec.inta)
+				rtl_write_dword(rtlpriv, 0x00B4, hisr & ~intvec.inta);
+			if (hisre & ~intvec.intb)
+				rtl_write_dword(rtlpriv, 0x00BC, hisre & ~intvec.intb);
+			rtl_pci_dbg.ne_cleared |= hisr;
+		}
 		return ret;
 	}
 
